{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793741545,"nonce":"pHH/hdXKcDHk6335yM6nN+ANu5o=","title":"Interpreter: compact typed scalar registers and bytecode","message":"Follow-up to 02245c49b6668b5842c75a7826d73fb8cf24e31aca8693d299ffa3311ba2ba99. NUC release layout measurements: ZyntaxValue is 72 bytes and Op is 40 bytes. Ordinary HIR integers, floats and pointers occupy full host-facing values. Arithmetic dispatch reads their tags and retags results, while frame initialization and drops touch the wide enum. The old \u003c=16-byte opcode documentation was inaccurate.\n\nExplore a compact internal scalar register representation using the HIR's known types, with host-facing ZyntaxValue conversion at API/FFI boundaries and separate owned storage for aggregate/dynamic cases. Preserve integer width/signedness, f32 rounding, undef detection, vector semantics, pointer/int round trips, GC scanning of integer-encoded pointers, and OSR live-in layout. Measure dispatch, setup costs and code/data-cache footprint before adopting it; do not conflate layout reduction with an established speedup. Small typed operations or selective fused bytecodes can then reduce dispatches without running the full HIR optimization pipeline at interpreter entry.\n\nThe unchanged scalar-loop control in the symbol-cache experiment runs at about 41 ns/iteration on the NUC; symbol caching does not improve its arithmetic dispatch. Source audit also finds as_vector cloning arrays for read-only SIMD consumers. Borrowing those inputs is a smaller independent opportunity, but has not been profiled against production kernels.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-interpreter-speed/{profile.out,results.json,candidate-profile.txt}. No changes to register representation or bytecode dispatch have been implemented in this investigation.","files":null}]}